Looking at the comment, it states a conditional (premise: `c != null`) with a universal quantifier over valid indices of this list, comparing the element of this list to the element of collection `c` at the same index.

<code>assert c != null => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.get(i));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert c != null => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]);</code>
--------------------
Looking at the comment, I need to express: if `c` is not null, then for every valid index `i` (0 to size), the element at index `i` in this list equals the element at index `i` in collection `c`.

The valid indices are `0 <= i < this.size()`. To access elements of collection `c`, I use `c.toArray()[i]`.

<code>assert c != null => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert c != null => (\forall int i; 0<=i && i<this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]));</code>
--------------------
Looking at the comment, it states a premise (c is not null) implies a forall over valid indices comparing this list's elements to collection c's elements.

<code>assert c != null => \forall int i; 0<=i && i<this.size(); this.get(i).equals(c.toArray()[i]);</code>
